        "html": "<div class=\"reading-card\"><h3>The side entrance · original practice text</h3><p class=\"source\">Fiction written for this course (2026). Not an extract from Orwell, Kureishi or Smith. Cite paragraph numbers.</p><div class=\"practice-text\"><p><b>§1</b> The community hall had two entrances, but the organisers had decorated only the front one. Blue ribbons crossed its glass doors beneath a banner that read EVERYONE BELONGS. At the side entrance, where my mother usually arrived with buckets, the light had stopped working. I had reported it twice. That evening I was wearing a volunteer’s badge, and somehow the badge made the broken light seem like somebody else’s responsibility.</p><p><b>§2</b> Inside, the coordinator handed me a speech. “Keep it cheerful, Leila. People have come to celebrate.” In the draft, our neighbourhood was a family. Its streets were bridges. Its future was bright. I searched for the people who cleaned those streets before sunrise, but they had not found their way into the sentences. My mother’s name appeared nowhere. Mine appeared at the bottom, beneath the word representative.</p><p><b>§3</b> At home, she had asked what I would say. I told her that the hall was giving young people a voice. “A voice to say what?” she asked. I laughed, although nothing was funny. Then she returned to ironing my shirt. I wanted to tell her that being invited mattered, that somebody had finally noticed us. Instead I thanked her for removing a stain from the collar.</p><p><b>§4</b> By seven, the hall was full. From the platform I could see the councillor, the donors and my history teacher. Beyond them stood my mother, still in her work shoes. The coordinator pointed at the microphone and smiled. I had practised the opening until the words no longer felt like words. Our neighbourhood is a family. A family looks after its own. A family leaves no one outside.</p><p><b>§5</b> Then the side door opened. A cleaner pushed a trolley through it, struggling to hold the heavy door with one foot. Two guests moved their chairs just far enough to let the trolley pass, then turned back towards the stage. Nobody was cruel. Nobody shouted. Nobody looked for long. The wheels squeaked through the silence, a small sound that suddenly seemed louder than the music.</p><p><b>§6</b> I looked down at the speech. There was a sentence about opportunities, another about gratitude, another about the importance of giving something back. I had been given three minutes. Three minutes to be visible; three minutes to make invisibility sound like a success. If I changed the words, the coordinator might never invite me again. If I kept them, my mother would hear exactly how much an invitation had cost.</p><p><b>§7</b> I began with the first line. My voice sounded confident through the speakers, which surprised me. Then I stopped. I said that families sometimes failed to notice who did the work. I said that the side entrance needed a light. These were small sentences, without bridges or bright futures. A few people clapped before I had finished. The coordinator did not.</p><p><b>§8</b> Afterwards, the councillor promised to mention the light at the next meeting. My teacher called me brave. My mother said the speech had been clear, then asked me to carry a box to the kitchen. On the way out I passed the banner again. Nothing on it had changed. I could not decide whether I had opened a door or merely described one. Behind me, somebody was already folding the chairs.</p></div></div><details class=\"scaffold\"><summary>Summary checklist</summary><p>Include the invitation, the prepared message, Leila’s recognition of invisible work, her change to the speech and the limited outcome. Do not add symbolism, quotations or your own judgement. A summary follows the task’s focus; it is not a list of every event.</p></details>"
